<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    ic_download — download glyph (media viewer, media bubbles)
    ===
    Referenced by:
      - activity_media_viewer.xml (btn_download top-bar action)
      - future: document message bubbles (Phase 7 — "tap to
        download")
      - future: audio message bubbles (Phase 7 — same)

    Constraints this file honors:

    1. MONOCHROME TEMPLATE.
       Single fill color (#FFFFFFFF), tinted at the consumer
       level. In the media viewer, the tint is
       @android:color/white (hardcoded) because the icon
       sits on a fixed semi-transparent black overlay.
       In a future bubble context, the tint will be a
       theme attribute (e.g., ?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant).
       Do NOT set a brand color here — the fill is a
       template that is tinted differently per consumer.

    2. 24dp VIEWPORT.
       Standard Material icon viewport. The media viewer's
       top-bar ImageButton declares 48dp × 48dp, and its
       Material 3 ImageButton default icon size is 24dp
       (centered in the 48dp touch target) — the vector
       renders at 24dp losslessly. Future bubble usage at
       16dp–20dp also scales cleanly.

    3. STANDARD MATERIAL "DOWNLOAD" GLYPH.
       This is Google's canonical Material Design "download"
       icon: an arrow pointing down, resting on a tray/line
       at the bottom. It is NOT a custom design. Every
       browser, file manager, and media app uses essentially
       this shape, and users recognize it instantly as
       "save to this device."

    4. SINGLE FILLED PATH.
       The arrow and the tray are drawn as a single closed
       path — the arrowhead merges into the horizontal line
       at the bottom, producing one continuous silhouette
       under nonZero fill. No evenOdd, no cutouts: this
       glyph is a solid shape at every point.

    5. NO androidx.AutoMirrored.
       The download glyph is vertically symmetric about
       its center (arrow centered on the tray). Mirroring
       under RTL produces no visible change. Do not add
       auto-mirroring.

    Rendering result at 24dp:
      A downward arrow onto a horizontal tray — instantly
      readable as "download / save to device."

    ============================================================
    Why download arrow (not save-to-folder, not floppy disk):
    ============================================================
      The "download" metaphor (arrow into a tray) is
      universally understood as "fetch from a remote source
      to this device." A "save" metaphor (floppy disk or
      a folder) implies "move an existing local file" or
      "write to a project," neither of which fits the media
      viewer's action ("fetch this remote image to my
      phone"). The Material design team's own media-viewer
      guidelines specify the download glyph for exactly
      this use case, so using it also matches what users
      expect from every other Android app that has a
      full-screen viewer.
    ============================================================
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <!--
        Canonical Material Design "download" path.

        Breakdown:
          M 5,20               start of the tray (left end)
          h 14                 tray's horizontal line (14
                               units wide)
          v 2                  tray's right edge (2 units
                               tall)
          H 5                  back across the tray's top
          z                    close (implicit line from
                               (5,22) to (5,20))

          M 12,3               top-center of the arrow
                               (start of the arrow
                               silhouette)
          ... (see the actual path data below for the
              full canonical construction)

        Full path data below is Google's official
        material-icons "download" asset. It produces a
        solid 2-unit-thick tray at the bottom (y=20 to
        y=22) and an arrow that descends from y=3 to
        y=15, with a triangular head centered at x=12.
        The arrowhead's width (from x=7 to x=17) matches
        the tray's width (from x=5 to x=19), producing
        the balanced "big arrow, thin tray" look that is
        the standard Material download icon.
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:pathData="M19,9h-4V3H9v6H5l7,7 7,-7zM5,18v2h14v-2H5z" />

</vector>
